select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d09', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — spiral the toolkit", "markdown": "Today's CPA practice mixes the kinematics moves you've built — position, velocity, acceleration, the equations of motion. The honors demand isn't to get more answers. It's to **say why your chosen equation was the right one**, and to make every hidden assumption stand in the open."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "warning", "title": "Name the assumption", "markdown": "Almost every kinematics equation you reach for carries a silent claim: **constant acceleration over the interval.** $v = v_0 + at$ and $\\Delta x = v_0 t + \\tfrac12 a t^2$ are only true if $a$ doesn't change while the clock runs.\n\nBefore you plug in, ask: *is $a$ actually constant here, or am I assuming it?* On a problem where it isn't (a car that brakes, then coasts, then accelerates), one equation across the whole trip is a lie. You'd have to break the motion into intervals where the assumption holds."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Solve, then name what you assumed\n\nWork the CPA problem set as written. But for **each** problem, before you trust the number, write one line in the margin: *the assumption I made to use this equation is ___.* Constant acceleration? A clean start from rest? Motion in a straight line so I can treat position as a single signed number? Most kinematics errors aren't arithmetic — they're an assumption that didn't actually hold."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "I chose the equation ___ for this problem. It was the right choice because the givens were ___ and the unknown was ___. The assumption it rests on is ___, and I know that assumption holds here because ___.", "prompt": "JUSTIFY (homework): Pick the hardest problem from today's set. Don't just show the work — defend the *choice* of equation. Why that one and not another? What assumption does it carry, and how do you know that assumption is safe for this particular problem?"}, {"id": "h5", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "If I had to predict 2026-XJ's position from its current velocity, the kinematics assumption I'd be leaning on is ___. It could fail because ___.", "prompt": "TRANSFER (homework): Soon you'll predict where 2026-XJ is at graduation from its velocity today. Which kinematics assumption would that prediction rest on — and name one reason it might quietly break over a year-long flight."}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d10', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — three views, one motion", "markdown": "You've now built three ways to describe the same moving thing: a **graph** (position or velocity vs. time), an **equation** (the kinematics relations), and a **vector** (magnitude plus direction). The honors move today is to prove to yourself they're not three topics — they're one idea in three costumes."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Connect backward — tie the three together\n\nLook back across the last week and make the seams explicit:\n\n- The **slope** of a position–time graph *is* the velocity in your equations *is* the magnitude-and-sign of your velocity vector. Same number, three faces.\n- The **slope** of a velocity–time graph *is* the $a$ in $v = v_0 + at$ *is* how the velocity vector is changing.\n- The **area** under a velocity–time graph *is* the $\\Delta x$ that $\\Delta x = v_0 t + \\tfrac12 a t^2$ computes.\n\nIf you can read a value off the graph, you can find it from the equation, and you can point it as a vector. A motion you can only handle one way you don't really own yet — you've memorized a procedure, not understood the motion."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "On a velocity–time graph, the slope tells me ___ and the area tells me ___. The same two facts show up in my equations as ___ and ___, and as a vector they look like ___.", "prompt": "JUSTIFY (homework): Take one velocity–time graph from today. Read its slope and its area. Then show that the *same* two quantities fall out of the kinematics equations, and describe what each one is as a vector. Make the case that the graph, the equation, and the vector are telling one story — not three."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "tip", "title": "Close forward", "markdown": "You can now fully describe *how* something moves. What you still can't say is *why* it moves that way — what makes the velocity change at all. That 'why' has a name, and it's the next thing we build: **force.** Hold onto the question."}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d11', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — build the equation, don't receive it", "markdown": "CPA varies mass (Inv 1.2), varies force (Inv 1.3), and sees that $F = ma$ falls out of the data. Today you do that **and then refuse to stop there.** You're going to derive the joint law yourself, and find the assumption hiding inside it."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Derive $F = ma$ from two single-variable experiments\n\nFrom the lab you hold two separate, one-variable claims:\n\n- **Inv 1.3 (vary force, mass fixed):** doubling the force doubled the acceleration. So at constant mass, $a \\propto F$.\n- **Inv 1.2 (vary mass, force fixed):** doubling the mass halved the acceleration. So at constant force, $a \\propto \\dfrac{1}{m}$.\n\nNow the honors step CPA skips. Each experiment only changed *one* variable. To put them together into one law, $a \\propto \\dfrac{F}{m}$, you have to make a claim neither experiment tested directly: that **force and mass act on acceleration independently** — that changing one doesn't secretly change how the other behaves. The combined law is the *simplest* relationship consistent with both lines, not a forced logical deduction. Write it as an equality and you get $a = k\\,\\dfrac{F}{m}$."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "tip", "title": "Setting the constant to 1 *defines* the newton", "markdown": "That $k$ isn't handed to you by nature — **you choose it.** Physicists set $k = 1$, which collapses the law to $F = ma$. But that choice isn't free: once $k = 1$, the force that gives a 1 kg mass an acceleration of 1 m/s² *must* equal exactly 1 force-unit. That force-unit is what we name the **newton**. You didn't just build the equation — by picking the constant, you defined the unit it's measured in."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "vocab", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "terms": [{"term": "newton", "definition": "the SI unit of force; 1 N is the force that gives a 1 kg mass an acceleration of 1 m/s², the unit fixed by choosing k = 1 in F = ma", "cognate": "newton"}, {"term": "proportionality constant", "definition": "the multiplier k that turns a proportionality (a ∝ F/m) into an equation (a = kF/m); its value is a unit choice, not a law of nature", "cognate": "constante de proporcionalidad / constante de proporcionalidade"}]}, {"id": "h5", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "My a-vs-F line and my a-vs-(1/m) line don't have the same slope, but they're both evidence for F = ma because ___. The independence assumption is ___, and I know force and mass act independently because ___.", "prompt": "JUSTIFY (homework): Your a-vs-F line and your a-vs-(1/m) line have different slopes. Explain why two single-variable lines can still justify one joint law, F = ma. Then a classmate objects: 'you just *assumed* force and mass act independently — how do you actually know they do?' Answer them honestly. What in the data, or in further experiment, would let you defend that independence?"}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d12', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — interrogate what you built", "markdown": "Yesterday you derived $F = ma$ and defined the newton. A model you can't attack you don't really trust. Today you go after your own equation: where does the experiment lie, and what does the equation quietly assume?"}, {"id": "h2", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "misconception", "title": "Your 'constant force' wasn't constant", "markdown": "The force in the vary-force lab came from a **hanging mass** pulling the cart. But here's the flaw: that hanging mass is *accelerating downward too* — it's part of the moving system, so the tension pulling your cart is **less than** the hanging weight, and it isn't truly fixed as you change the cart's load.\n\nSo the 'constant force' label on your data is an approximation, not a fact. Think about which way that flaw would bend your $a$-vs-$F$ line — and why CPA can use the data anyway while honors has to name the error."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Where else does the equation strain?\n\nTwo more cracks worth seeing before you walk away trusting $F = ma$:\n\n- **Friction and the track** ate some of your force, so the *net* force on the cart was a little less than what you applied — a systematic offset, not random scatter.\n- **$F = ma$ assumes the mass is fixed.** It's silent about what happens if the moving thing *throws mass off* while it accelerates. Nothing in your derivation covers that case — the law simply doesn't apply when $m$ changes mid-motion."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "F = ma assumes the mass stays fixed. A real asteroid deflection works by ___, which throws mass off — so F = ma alone can't handle it. The later unit that does is ___, because ___.", "prompt": "TRANSFER (homework): $F = ma$ assumes a fixed mass. But a real deflection mission moves an asteroid by *throwing mass off* it — ejecting material so the rest recoils. That breaks the fixed-mass assumption you found today. Name the later unit that has to handle changing-mass problems, and explain why $F = ma$ by itself can't."}, {"id": "h5", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "tip", "title": "Close forward", "markdown": "You now own the most important equation of the year — *and* you've located the two assumptions hiding inside it: fixed mass, and a clean isolated force. Units 3 and 4 exist to attack exactly those two assumptions. You already know where they'll strike."}, {"id": "h6", "type": "marzano", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "targetId": "u1.d12-fma-limits"}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d13', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — the cancellation trap", "markdown": "Newton's third law says forces come in equal-and-opposite pairs. Every student's first instinct is the same wrong one: *if they're equal and opposite, they cancel — so nothing should ever move.* The honors demand today is to defend, in prose, exactly why that reasoning fails."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Why the pair doesn't cancel\n\nHere's the key the CPA packet states and honors must *defend*: the two forces in a third-law pair **act on different objects.** When you push a wall, you push the wall and the wall pushes you. Those forces are equal and opposite — but one acts on the wall and one acts on you.\n\nForces only cancel when they act on the **same** object — that's what 'net force' means. A third-law pair never acts on the same object, so it *can't* cancel. To find whether *you* accelerate, you add up only the forces on *you* (the wall's push, friction, your weight) — the force you exert on the wall isn't in that sum at all, because it isn't acting on you. Confusing 'equal and opposite' (third law, two objects) with 'balanced' (net force, one object) is the single most common dynamics error."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "The two forces in a Newton's-third-law pair don't cancel because ___. Forces only cancel when ___, but a third-law pair always ___. So 'equal and opposite means it can't move' is wrong because ___.", "prompt": "JUSTIFY (homework): A classmate insists: 'Newton's third law forces are equal and opposite, so they cancel — nothing should ever be able to accelerate.' Take a concrete example (a rocket, a swimmer, you pushing off a wall) and defend in a paragraph why the equal-and-opposite pair does NOT cancel, and how something still accelerates. Be precise about *which object* each force acts on."}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d14', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — a diagram is an argument", "markdown": "A free-body diagram isn't decoration — it's a claim about *which* forces are real and *why*. CPA draws the arrows correctly. Honors has to **justify every arrow on the page**: name the source of each force, and name what you assumed away by leaving other things off."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Justify each force you draw\n\nFor every arrow on your free-body diagram, you should be able to answer two questions:\n\n1. **What object is pushing or pulling here?** Every force has a source. Weight comes from Earth's gravity. The normal force comes from the surface. Tension comes from the string. Friction comes from the surface resisting sliding. If you can't name the *other object* exerting it, the arrow doesn't belong.\n2. **Is this force acting on *my* object?** The FBD shows only the forces *on* the body — never the forces the body exerts on other things (those live on *their* diagrams).\n\nAn arrow you can't source is a guess. A missing arrow is a force you forgot. Both are arguments you'd lose."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "warning", "title": "State your assumptions", "markdown": "Every clean free-body diagram is built on idealizations you should say out loud:\n\n- **Massless string** — so tension is the same all along it, and the string adds no weight of its own.\n- **Frictionless surface** — drawn only when you've *decided* friction is small enough to ignore (a modeling choice, not a fact).\n- **Point particle** — the object is treated as a dot with all forces acting at one point, so you can ignore rotation and how the object is shaped.\n\nThese assumptions make the diagram solvable. They also make it *wrong* in situations where they don't hold. An honors FBD names them; it doesn't smuggle them in."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "On my free-body diagram I drew the forces ___. Each one is justified because its source is ___. I left off ___ because I assumed ___, which is safe here since ___.", "prompt": "JUSTIFY (homework): Draw the free-body diagram for one scenario from today (a box on an incline, a hanging sign, a cart pulled by a string). Then write a paragraph defending it: name the *source* of every arrow you drew, and name every idealization you assumed (massless string, frictionless, point particle) and why each is reasonable here."}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d15', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — friction is a choice you make", "markdown": "Back on Day 1 you learned a model is a claim that can have limits. Friction is where that idea pays off. CPA computes friction forces. Honors treats friction as a **modeling decision**: sometimes you include it, sometimes you neglect it — and you must be able to defend which, and when."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "warning", "title": "Neglecting friction is a modeling choice, not a fact", "markdown": "Every time you wrote 'frictionless' on a diagram, you made a **choice** — you decided friction was small enough next to the other forces to throw away. That choice is sometimes brilliant and sometimes a lie.\n\n- **Appropriate** when friction is genuinely tiny compared to what's driving the motion: a puck on fresh ice, a spacecraft coasting through near-vacuum, a cart on a low-friction track over a short run.\n- **A lie** when friction is doing real work in the answer: a car braking to a stop (friction *is* the stopping force), a box that won't slide until you push hard enough, anything reaching terminal velocity.\n\nThe honest move isn't 'always include friction' or 'always ignore it.' It's: *estimate how big friction is relative to everything else, then decide — and say you decided.*"}, {"id": "h3", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## When does the simplification lie?\n\nThe test for whether neglecting friction is safe isn't a rule — it's a comparison. Ask: **is the friction force small compared to the other forces in the problem, AND is friction not the very thing the question is about?** If friction is small *and* peripheral, drop it and gain a clean, solvable model. If friction is large, or if it's the answer the problem is hunting (how far does the car skid? will the box slip?), then 'frictionless' deletes the physics that matters and your answer is confidently wrong."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "Neglecting friction is appropriate when ___, because ___. It lies when ___, because ___. The test I use to decide is ___.", "prompt": "JUSTIFY (homework): Give one situation where neglecting friction is a smart, defensible modeling choice, and one where it badly distorts the answer. For each, explain *why* using the size of friction relative to the other forces and whether friction is central to the question. Then state the general test you'd use to decide before solving any new problem."}] $honors$::jsonb);
